30517578125, 0.01557159423828125, -0.0260772705078125, -0.0025177001953125, -0.00949859619140625, 0.0004987716674804688, -0.00257110595703125, 0.01611328125, -0.031402587890625, 0.017425537109375, 0.0212860107421875, 0.006267547607421875, 0.00016164779663085938, -0.0197906494140625, 0.005992889404296875, -0.0208740234375, 0.0192718505859375, -0.020294189453125, -0.0041961669921875, -0.0141754150390625, -0.03021240234375, -0.001708984375, -0.0118255615234375, 0.004810333251953125, -0.01291656494140625, 0.0408935546875, 0.00521087646484375, 0.0175018310546875, -0.026641845703125, 0.0018253326416015625, 0.0123138427734375, 0.00556182861328125, 0.003582000732421875, -0.0014467239379882812, -0.02886962890625, 0.0108795166015625, -0.02398681640625, 0.0007338523864746094, -0.00473785400390625, -0.0196075439453125, 0.0216217041015625, -0.057098388671875, -0.0046234130859375, -0.0267181396484375, -0.02606201171875, 0.01776123046875, -0.0350341796875, -0.0095672607421875, -0.009368896484375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability aspects of these methods have been critically overlooked. In this work, we address the following research questions: (1) How robust are memory-based self-improving agents to stochasticity and task order? (2) What factors contribute to their observed fragility, and how can evaluation protocols be improved? To this end, we conduct a comprehensive re-evaluation of two representative memory-based methods: Episodic Memory Augmented Agents (EMAA) and Continual Prompt Memory (CPM). \n\nOur methodology broadens the scope of evaluation along two axes. First, we include multiple runs (five per configuration) to quantify variance. Second, we randomly shuffle the order of tasks to investigate the effect of task order. Experiments are conducted on two complex multi-step environments: a text-based navigation domain and a procedural puzzle-solving suite, each comprising 10\u201320 tasks of varying difficulty. \n\nThrough these experiments, we make two key observations that expose the fragility of current methods. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks; stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, as evidenced by standard deviations in success rates reaching up to 18% across runs. Second, the agent\u2019s improvement is highly dependent on task order: for example, shuffling tasks led to performance drops of up to 25% compared to the default curriculum order. Prior works often adopt default orderings that impose an implicit curriculum, acting as a hidden prerequisite for success.\n\nTo better understand this fragility, we manually examine the agents\u2019 memory by reviewing stored trajectories and decision rationales, using a rubric to assess completeness, relevance, and consistency. Two independent annotators performed this analysis, and disagreements were resolved through discussion. We hypothesize that task and environment underspecification contribute to this fragility. We validate this hypothesis by incorporating information that enables better specification, such as detailed rubrics and environment feedback, into the memory construction process. While this added information partially closes the performance degradation observed in previous experiments (reducing the average performance gap by 10\u201312%), significant gaps still remain, with agents failing on tasks requiring nuanced context or long-term dependencies.\n\nThese findings suggest that other uncharacterized factors\u2014such as limitations in memory retrieval mechanisms, insufficient generalization across tasks, or brittleness to environment stochasticity\u2014may also contribute to fragility. Future work should investigate these possibilities, for example by analyzing memory retrieval failures or testing with adversarial task sequences.\n\nLooking ahead, our work advocates for more rigorous evaluation protocols for self-improving agents, such as reporting results across multiple random seeds, varying task orders, and including ablation studies on memory content. Moreover, our findings on underspecification call for systems and interfaces that enable effective human oversight, such as interactive memory editing tools or real-time feedback dashboards, to prevent agents from failing in unforeseeable ways."}}